WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.240
Okay.

00:00.240 --> 00:03.200
So now first let's have a look again to the database structure.

00:03.200 --> 00:07.000
We have learned that in each SQL server there are multiple databases.

00:07.000 --> 00:09.600
And in each database there are multiple schemas.

00:09.640 --> 00:15.720
And now inside each schema we can define multiple objects like we can define tables and views.

00:15.720 --> 00:18.560
And now we will be focusing on the object table.

00:18.560 --> 00:23.960
And we have learned as well we can use the language DDL Data Definition language, which is a set of

00:23.960 --> 00:27.240
SQL commands in order to define this database structure.

00:27.240 --> 00:33.960
So we can use the SQL command create in order to define a new table or altar, in order to update the

00:33.960 --> 00:37.120
structure or drop in order to drop the whole table.

00:37.120 --> 00:40.400
So a table is an object in the database structure.

00:40.400 --> 00:41.480
And we have learned as well.

00:41.480 --> 00:44.160
There is three levels of the database architecture.

00:44.160 --> 00:50.480
And we have understood that at the logical level, the middle one, the conceptual level, we deal as

00:50.520 --> 00:54.160
application developer or data engineer with the tables.

00:54.160 --> 00:57.000
So we define tables and relationship between them.

00:57.000 --> 01:02.160
So if you are an end user or a business analyst it's going to be little bit more hard to work with the

01:02.160 --> 01:02.760
tables.

01:02.760 --> 01:05.200
You have to be a developer or a data engineer.

01:05.400 --> 01:11.760
But working with tables is way easier than working with the complexity of the database at the physical

01:11.760 --> 01:12.160
level.

01:12.160 --> 01:16.960
So you don't have to be a database expert or administrator to work with tables.

01:16.960 --> 01:22.400
So the difficulty here is like in the middle, the abstraction is not that low, but as well not that

01:22.440 --> 01:22.800
high.

01:22.840 --> 01:25.520
So now let's answer the question what are tables?

01:25.560 --> 01:28.520
A database table is a structured collection of data.

01:28.520 --> 01:32.160
It's like a simple grid or spreadsheet that you might find in Excel.

01:32.160 --> 01:38.080
So it has different columns like each column represents a field like the ID name country.

01:38.080 --> 01:40.600
And the table has as well multiple rows.

01:40.640 --> 01:44.320
And each row represents a record or an entry of the data.

01:44.320 --> 01:50.640
So for example, if this table is about the employees then each record each row is one employee.

01:50.680 --> 01:54.640
Now the intersect between the rows and columns we call it a cell.

01:54.680 --> 01:57.320
And a cell is a single piece of data.

01:57.360 --> 02:02.720
Now the whole table can be stored physically in the database as database files.

02:02.720 --> 02:08.520
So they are in the database like multiple files that are holding the information about the table.

02:08.520 --> 02:13.280
And those files are stored physically in that disk storage of the database.

02:13.280 --> 02:18.950
So that means your data inside the tables are not stored like a spreadsheet like an Excel, but they

02:18.990 --> 02:25.550
are stored in special database files that usual developers and end users don't have access to those

02:25.550 --> 02:26.110
files.

02:26.110 --> 02:31.870
So tables again, it's like an abstraction and representation for the actual data that are in the files.

02:31.870 --> 02:36.790
So actually each time you are querying the database table, the database has to go to those files and

02:36.790 --> 02:38.070
fetch the data for you.

02:38.070 --> 02:38.470
All right.

02:38.470 --> 02:40.990
So this is what we mean with database tables.

02:44.990 --> 02:45.310
Okay.

02:45.350 --> 02:48.150
So now we have like different types of tables in SQL.

02:48.310 --> 02:50.430
We have tables that stays forever.

02:50.470 --> 02:52.670
We call it permanent tables.

02:52.670 --> 02:55.350
So they stay as long as you don't drop them.

02:55.350 --> 02:59.390
And you have another type of tables they called the temporary tables.

02:59.390 --> 03:03.550
And those tables can be deleted and dropped once the session ends.

03:03.550 --> 03:06.510
So now we're going to focus first on the first type that permanent tables.

03:06.670 --> 03:09.310
And there are two ways on how to create them.

03:09.310 --> 03:15.070
The first way is the classical way where you create a table from the scratch, and then you go and insert

03:15.070 --> 03:15.870
your data.

03:15.870 --> 03:21.070
So we call it create, insert, and the other way called create Table.

03:21.110 --> 03:25.990
As select it can create as well a table but based on SQL query.

03:25.990 --> 03:28.070
So let's understand the differences between them.
